siddhānta* into Arabic as *Kitāb al-Sindhind al-Kabīr* in the late 8th century, introducing Indian astronomy to the Islamic world."}, {"sourceSlug": "muammad-ibn-ibrhm-al-fazr", "sourceName": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī", "verb": "OCCURS_IN", "targetSlug": "baghdad", "targetName": "Baghdad", "context": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī's significant astronomical and mathematical work occurred primarily in Baghdad, the intellectual center of the Abbasid Caliphate, during the late 8th century CE."}, {"sourceSlug": "al-khwarizmi", "sourceName": "Muḥammad ibn Mūsā al-Khwārizmī", "verb": "INFLUENCES", "targetSlug": "islamic-astronomy", "targetName": "Islamic Astronomy", "context": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī's *Sindhind* translation and astronomical tables significantly influenced later Islamic astronomers, including al-Khwārizmī, who built upon his work in the 9th century."}], "places": [{"name": "Kufa, Iraq", "role": "Birthplace"}, {"name": "Baghdad, Iraq", "role": "Center of activity"}, {"name": "India", "role": "Source of knowledge"}], "texts": [{"title": "Wikipedia: Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī", "type": "Reference article", "slug": "muḥammad-ibn-ibrāhīm-al-fazārī"}], "externalLinks": ["https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q584679", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mu%E1%B8%A5ammad_ibn_Ibr%C4%81h%C4%ABm_al-Faz%C4%81r%C4%AB"], "tags": ["Logicians & Mathematicians", "innovation-and-technology", "cause-and-effect", "temporal-linkage"], "thumbnailUrl": "", "quote": "", "legacySummary": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī left a notable mark on Medieval history, documented across 0 Wikipedia editions worldwide. Key lasting impacts include: Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars; Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati. Their legacy is studied through the lens of historical causation.", "historicalSignificance": {"significanceScore": 7, "significanceNarrative": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī was a crucial figure in the early Islamic Golden Age, establishing the foundations of Islamic astronomy through his pivotal translation of the *Sindhind* and the creation of the first Islamic astrolabe, directly influencing subsequent generations of scholars.", "significanceCategory": "regional"}, "_editLog": [{"timestamp": "2026-11-03T08:19:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41601624940", "field": "summary", "oldValue": "Muhammad ibn Ibrahim ibn Habib ibn Sulayman ibn Samra ibn Jundab al-Fazari was an Arab philosopher, mathematician and astronomer.", "newValue": "Muḥammad ibn Ibrāhīm al-Fazārī (born c. 746 CE, Kufa; died c. 806 CE) was a pivotal Arab polymath whose work at the dawn of the Abbasid Golden Age fundamentally shaped Islamic scientific tradition, particularly in astronomy and instrument-making. Operating under the patronage of Caliph al-Manṣūr and later Hārūn al-Rashīd, al-Fazārī was instrumental in transmitting and synthesizing sophisticated Indian and Hellenistic astronomical knowledge into the nascent Islamic intellectual sphere, thereby laying critical groundwork for centuries of scientific advancement.

His most significant contributions include the construction of the first astrolabe in the Islamic world, a complex instrument vital for celestial navigation and timekeeping, and his pivotal role in translating the Sanskrit astronomical treatise *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta* by Brahmagupta. This translation, known as *Kitāb al-Sindhind al-Kabīr*, was a watershed moment, introducing sophisticated Indian mathematical and astronomical concepts, including positional notation and the use of zero, to the Islamic world. He also authored several astronomical tables (zijes) that synthesized these diverse traditions, further integrating them into Abbasid scholarly practice.

Al-Fazārī's work had profound and lasting consequences, effectively establishing the foundational curriculum and methodology for Islamic astronomy. The *Sindhind* became the most influential astronomical text in the Islamic world for over a century, directly impacting subsequent scholars like al-Khwārizmī, whose own zijes were built upon al-Fazārī's translations and interpretations. His pioneering efforts in instrument design spurred the development of a rich tradition of Islamic scientific instrumentation, making Baghdad a global center for astronomical observation and innovation."}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-03T08:19:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41601624940", "field": "detailsJson.causes", "oldValue": [{"title": "Medieval era: interplay of religious authority, feudal governance, and trade network expansion", "type": "EventWindow", "year": "746 CE, Kufa"}, {"title": "Accumulation of mathematical knowledge and formal systems", "type": "Idea", "year": "746 CE, Kufa"}], "newValue": ["The Abbasid Caliphate's patronage of science under figures like al-Manṣūr created a fertile environment for scholars like al-Fazārī.", "The availability of Indian astronomical texts, particularly the *Brahmasphuṭasiddhānta*, provided advanced mathematical and astronomical concepts for translation and study.", "The existing Hellenistic scientific traditions in the Middle East offered a framework for integrating and developing new astronomical instruments and theories."]}, {"timestamp": "2026-11-03T08:19:07Z", "editorId": "gemini-2.5-flash·cloud·GH#41601624940", "field": "detailsJson.effects", "oldValue": [{"title": "Advanced mathematical knowledge used by subsequent scholars", "type": "Idea", "year": "806 CE"}, {"title": "Contributed to synthesis of classical and religious thought informing Renaissance and Reformati", "type": "Movement", "year": "806 CE"}], "newValue": ["Al-Fazārī's translation of the *Sindhind* introduced sophisticated Indian astronomy and mathematics, includ